WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.360
- I'm Amara, I'm a student at Dartmouth College

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.860
and I love to code and dance.

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:16.063
People always thought I was good with technical stuff with computers and phones and then I did

00:00:16.063 --> 00:00:19.943
Girl Suit Code and yeah seeing other girls interested and realizing that it was more

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:25.927
accessible than I think I thought was a good gateway into computer science.

00:00:25.927 --> 00:00:29.887
Welcome to Game Lab. Game Lab is a tool that lets you drop

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:35.031
pictures, design animations, and build your own games with code.

00:00:35.031 --> 00:00:38.951
To create these animations and games, you'll be learning and using the programming language

00:00:38.951 --> 00:00:39.911
JavaScript.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:45.255
GameLab is designed with a beginning programmer in mind, but it also includes many powerful

00:00:45.255 --> 00:00:47.735
features to help you bring your creations to life.

00:00:47.735 --> 00:00:48.735
Let's get started!

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:57.919
In Game Lab, you can add lines of code to your program by dragging blocks out from the toolbox and snapping them together.

00:00:57.919 --> 00:00:59.903
Then click the Run button to see the results.

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:04.283
the result. The output of the program will show up on the left in the display

00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:09.887
area. If you prefer, you can also type the commands yourself.

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:15.027
Either way you write your code, you can always flip between block mode and text mode.

00:01:15.027 --> 00:01:19.827
In fact, you can work in text mode and drag blocks out to insert the text.

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:24.871
If you make an error, you can use the blocks as a way to check if it's the right command.

00:01:24.871 --> 00:01:29.271
If you want to get rid of a line of code, just drag the block off the screen.

00:01:29.271 --> 00:01:29.855
You can also delete the block.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:33.495
delete it from the text.

00:01:33.495 --> 00:01:37.615
To start out, you'll be drawing with simple shapes like rectangles and ellipses.

00:01:37.615 --> 00:01:38.895
That's a fancy name for an oval.

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:45.159
To change where shapes show up on the screen, you can change the numbers given to each command.

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:48.359
The first two numbers set the position of the shape.

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:49.823
The last two numbers change the size.

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:55.423
Let's check out an example where we want to move our rectangle down the screen and

00:01:55.423 --> 00:01:57.603
then make it wider.

00:01:57.603 --> 00:01:59.803
In GameLab, the X and Y position starts at the top.

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:04.687
the top left corner. If we want to move the rectangle down the screen, we actually need

00:02:04.687 --> 00:02:09.727
to increase the Y value. To make the rectangle wider, we'll also need to increase its width.

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:14.791
Learning how to use new commands doesn't have to be trial and error.

00:02:14.791 --> 00:02:19.775
If you hover over any of the blocks, you can read more about how to use it and see detailed examples.

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:21.775
of each command.

00:02:21.775 --> 00:02:23.775
I don't know what I want to do after college,

00:02:23.775 --> 00:02:28.775
but I know that computer science will give me a lot of opportunities to work in many different industries,

00:02:28.775 --> 00:02:29.759
which is why I like it.

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:31.759
Thank you so much.
